  {
    "title": "News for Veterans – July 2026",
    "url": "https://senatorfarry.com/enews/vets-0726/",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "News for Veterans - July 2026 - Senator Farry\nView this email in a browser\nIn this Update:\nPFC George Raymon Huntzinger Memorial Bridge Rededication\nPinning Ceremony for Lt. Colonel Horne\nDisplaying the POW/MIA Flag at Public Schools\nPA Guard Military Family Education Program\nBeware of the “Veterans Savings Program” Postcard Scam\nVeteran Benefits Assistance\nVeteran Burial Benefits\nNational Parks Military Lifetime Pass\nSave The Date for Our Senior Expo\nPFC George Raymond Huntzinger Memorial Bridge Rededication\nOn June 18, the Brownsville Road Bridge over Neshaminy Creek connecting Middletown and Lower Southampton townships reopened five months ahead of schedule and a $1 million less cost to taxpayers.\nPrior to the reopening, I joined Rep. Hogan, Matt Allen, Bucks County Veterans Affairs, Ed Preston, Chairman PA Vietnam Veterans Memorial Fund, Din Abazi, P.E., District Executive PennDOT, Kevin Loftus, President & CEO, Loftus Construction, local veterans and community members for a rededication ceremony in honor of PFC George Raymond Huntzinger, a fallen solder in the Vietnam War. The bridge was originally dedicated in August 2023 before PennDOT began construction to replace the bridge in spring 2025.\nHuntzinger, a Langhorne resident, began his tour of duty with the US army on September 11, 1969, and less than two months later on October 29\nth\nhe tragically lost his life on October while dismantling a landmine in Vietnam. PFC Huntzinger will forever be remembered and honored in our community.\nPinning Ceremony for Lt. Colonel Horne\nIt was an honor to attend the pinning ceremony celebrating Zachary Horne’s promotion to Lieutenant Colonel on July 4 at his family’s restaurant, The Irish Horne in Richboro.\nA proud Council Rock South graduate, Lt. Colonel Horne has served our nation for the past 17 years, including deployments to Afghanistan and assignments in Germany.\nThank you to Lt. Colonel Horne for his service, sacrifice, and unwavering dedication to our nation. It was a privilege to celebrate this special milestone with you, your family, and Rep. Kristin Marcell.\nDisplaying the POW/MIA Flag at Public Schools\nIn June, the Senate unanimously passed\nSenate Bill 835\n, which would require the display of the official POW/MIA flag when space permits, at public schools during times the U.S. flag is also flown.\nTo read more about the legislation, click here.\nPA Guard Military Family Education Program\nAs students begin preparing for the fall semester, Pennsylvania National Guard members are reminded their families could be eligible for assistance to offset the cost of higher education.\nThe Military Family Education Program is for Guard members who completed an initial service obligation on or after July 1, 2019, and are eligible to re-enlist in the PA National Guard for a period of six years.\nThe grants can be used for a degree-granting or certificate-granting curriculum, course of study, or training program required for entrance into a specific career at an approved Pennsylvania institution of higher learning.\nRead more details and find applications here\n.\nBeware of the “Veterans Savings Program” Postcard Scam\nVeterans and their spouses are being targeted by a new postcard scam advertising a fraudulent “Veterans Saving Program.” The mailer claims eligibility for an extra $185 monthly payment and dental benefits, but the program does not exist.\nMore information about this scam is available here.\nVeteran Benefits Assistance\nThe Pennsylvania Department of Military and Veterans Affairs (DMVA) provides veterans and their beneficiaries with free and safe assistance when applying for benefits. Seeking free help from the DMVA can help veterans avoid scams and exploitation from people looking to profit from veterans earned benefits.\nFor more information about pension poaching, click here.\nVeteran Burial Benefits\nEligible veterans and their spouses can receive no-cost burial benefits at U.S. Department of Veterans Affairs (VA) national cemeteries. These benefits include burial in a VA National Cemetery, headstone or marker with inscription included and gravesite maintenance in perpetuity.\nVeterans’ spouses are still eligible for these benefits if they pre-decease the veteran or if the veteran is interred in a non-VA cemetery.\nFor more information on veteran burial benefits from the VA, click here.\nNational Parks Military Lifetime Pass\nVeterans of the U.S. Armed Forces and members of Gold Star Families are eligible for a free Military Lifetime Pass at national parks, which will admit the passholder and accompanying passengers in a non-commercial vehicle at per vehicle fee areas, or the passholder and up to three additional people over the age of 16 where per person fees are charged.\nPasses cover entrance and day-use fees at lands managed by the National Park Service, the Bureau of Land Management, the Bureau of Reclamation, the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, the U.S. Forest Service and the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers.\nTo reques",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Senator Farry's July 2026 newsletter covers several veteran-related activities and information: he attended a bridge rededication ceremony honoring a fallen Vietnam War soldier and a military promotion ceremony, and he notes that the Pennsylvania Senate unanimously passed a bill requiring POW/MIA flags at public schools. The newsletter also shares informational resources for veterans including education grants, burial benefits, a national parks pass, and a warning about a postcard scam targeting veterans.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "other",
        "education",
        "public_safety"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
