  {
    "title": "[Read More]",
    "url": "https://senatorfarry.com/enews/073126-2/",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "E-news Updates - July 31, 2026 - Senator Farry\nE-news Updates - July 31, 2026 - Senator Farry\nView this email in a browser\nIn this Update:\nSenate Stands with Attorney General, District Attorneys, State Troopers and Police\nHonoring Dr. Widenmeyer for 48 Years of Exceptional Veterinary Care\nOver $2 Million in State Funding for Northampton Township\nInvesting in the Future of PA College Students\nNominate a Non-Teaching School Employee for a RISE Award\nOrgan and Tissue Donation Saves Lives\nSave The Date For our Upcoming Events\nNational Night Out in the 6th Senatorial District\nSenate Stands with Attorney General, District Attorneys, State Troopers and Police\nSecond-degree murder is a horrific crime – these are homicides committed while the defendant was also committing another violent felony such as rape, arson, robbery, burglary or kidnapping. Those who commit such an act deserve a punishment that matches.\nIn March, the Pennsylvania Supreme Court ruled that our current sentencing structure for second-degree murder (life without parole in every case) is unconstitutional. The justices gave the General Assembly 120 days to enact legislation correcting the situation.\nThe Senate\nsuccessfully passed strong bipartisan legislation to address this issue\n– twice. Once in June and again in early July. The Senate’s legislation is supported by Pennsylvania Attorney General Dave Sunday, Pennsylvania District Attorneys Association, the Pennsylvania State Troopers Association, the Pennsylvania Chiefs of Police Association, and Pennsylvania State Lodge Fraternal Order of Police.\nThe House refused to take action on this important issue until day 119 of the court-imposed 120 day deadline. Unfortunately, the majority leadership in the House of Representatives refused to allow a full House vote on this bipartisan legislation, instead in committee amended the bill to weaken it thereby taking away the opportunity for the full House of Representatives to vote on the issue. The House added a provision to the bill as amended that could allow more than half of all inmates who have not served their full sentence, including those on death row for killing children or a police officer, to qualify for release from prison under a weakly defined “medical release” program. As the House debated the bill, one side of the aisle seemed focused on relief for convicted murderers while the other focused on not revictimizing the family members whose loved one was murdered. The bill passed with nearly 100% of the vote on party lines.\nThe majority of the public was unaware of this issue and the deadline that the House failed to meet last week. Myself, and local representatives held a press conference last week to help make the public aware of the issue and most importantly to stand for the murder victim’s family members.\nHere is a link to the press conference\n. To watch my videos on the issue, you can visit my\nFacebook page\n.\nAs a result of the House Majority’s failures over 1,100 convicted murderers will now be able to seek their release including 17 from Bucks County and 545 from Philadelphia.\nHonoring Dr. Widenmeyer for 48 Years of Exceptional Veterinary Care\nCongratulations to Dr. Widenmeyer on his well-deserved retirement from practicing veterinary medicine after 48 years of dedicated service. I was proud to present him with a Pennsylvania Senate citation recognizing his remarkable career and commitment to our community.\nDr. Widenmeyer founded Flowers Mill Veterinary Hospital in 1977 and has spent decades providing compassionate, high-quality care to countless pets and the families who love them.\nThank you for your dedication, Dr. Widenmeyer. Wishing you all the best in this well-earned next chapter.\nOver $2 million in State Funding for Northampton Township\nPA State Rep. Kristin Marcell and I were proud to secure over $2.3 million in state grant funding to Northampton Township for critical infrastructure and pedestrian safety improvements that will benefit residents for years to come. We appreciate supporting Northampton Township’s efforts to improve pedestrian safety for its residents and students.\nThese investments include:\nA basin retrofit for the Tanner Estates neighborhood\nNew pedestrian light poles and safer sidewalks in the Village of Richboro\nADA upgrades and safety improvements at four school crossings\nDevelopment of a multi-use trail connecting neighborhoods to Tyler State Park\nInvesting in the Future of PA College Students\nThe successful Grow PA Scholarship program invests in Pennsylvania students looking to pursue a college education, develops the future of our workforce and incentivizes jobs to remain here in the commonwealth.\nImportantly, the program secures a commitment for students to stay and work in Pennsylvania communities after graduation, helping to address the state’s future economic and demographic challenges.\nWith demand rising for Grow PA scholarships, the Senate secured a $10 million funding increase for the program in the 20",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Senator Farry's newsletter covers several actions: the Pennsylvania Senate passed legislation twice to address a Supreme Court ruling on second-degree murder sentencing, though the House amended the bill in ways Farry opposes; Farry presented a retirement citation to a local veterinarian; secured $2.3 million in state grants for Northampton Township infrastructure projects; and highlights a $10 million funding increase for the Grow PA Scholarship program.",
      "action_type": "statement",
