  {
    "title": "E-news Updates – August 14, 2026",
    "url": "https://senatorfarry.com/enews/081426-2/",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "E-news Updates - August 14, 2026 - Senator Farry\nE-news Updates - August 14, 2026 - Senator Farry\nView this email in a browser\nIn this Update:\nUpcoming Events\nSupporting Our First Responders\nUpdates to U.S. 1 PennDOT Improvement Project\nUnderstanding Digital Threats to Children\nUpcoming Events\nHome Safety Event\nI hope you can join us for a Home Safety Event hosted by Kidde on August 29\nth\nfrom 11 am to 1 pm at Middle Bucks Institute of Technology, located at 2740 York Rd.in Jamison.\nAttendees will have the opportunity to refresh their fire and carbon monoxide safety knowledge, while enjoying a variety of family-friendly activities. I will be joined by Rep. Marcell and members of the Warwick Fire Department.\nThe event will feature fire and carbon monoxide safety education tools, a meet and greet with Peppa Pig, a snow cone truck, giveaways, and more. Free smoke detectors will be available to community members who need them.\nLearn More about Kidde and their Cause For Alarm Campaign\nSenior Expo\nWe invite seniors and their families of the 6\nth\nSenatorial District to our annual Senior Expo. The expo is a ‘one stop shop’ opportunity to gather valuable information and meet face to face with people in our area who can assist them with a wide range of services. It will be held on Thursday September 17 from 10 am to 1 pm at Northampton Valley Country Club.\nThe event will feature exhibitors from state, federal, county, and local agencies offering information on various programs available to assist seniors. There will also be health screenings, senior SEPTA key card processing station, and a prescription take-back on site.\nOur staff will be onsite to help with any state-related questions. We hope you can join us for this informative and fun day.\nBensalem Shredding Event\nRep. Tomlinson and I are hosting a Shredding Event on Saturday September 19 from 10 am to noon at Bensalem High School, located at 4319 Hulmeville Ave in Bensalem.\nWith the cases of identity theft rising at a significant rate, this is a great opportunity to properly dispose of documents containing personal information. Residents of the 6\nth\nSenatorial district are encouraged to bring documents that contain sensitive information, such as Social Security numbers, medical information, or bank account numbers, to be thoroughly shredded.\nDue to high demand, individuals are limited to two file boxes of paper to be shredded. Please do not place your paper in plastic bags as it cannot be shredded. This is an event for residents only, no businesses, please.\nPrior to the event, please place all shredding materials and donation items in the trunk of your vehicle.  Once you arrive, stay in your vehicle and staff will assist with removing the materials.\nAny questions about our upcoming events, please call our office at 215-638-1784\nor visit our website\nSupporting Our Local First Responders\nLast Tuesday night, I had the opportunity to visit several National Night Out events across the 6th Senatorial District. National Night Out is an annual event that brings police, first responders, and the community together to build stronger relationships and promote public safety. It was great to speak with so many of our local first responders and residents who make our community stronger.\nAs your state senator, I am proud to bring state funding back to our communities to support our first responders, helping to ease the burden on taxpayers while supporting the vital services our communities rely on. I have helped secure over $700,000 in state funding for our fire and EMS companies across the 6\nth\nDistrict. It was especially rewarding to see firsthand how these investments are being put to use in our communities.\nAt the Warminster Township National Night Out, I was proud to present a state funding check to the Warminster Fire Department to support the purchase of new AEDs for their Department. These lifesaving devices will help our firefighters and emergency responders provide critical care when seconds matter.\nAt the Bensalem National Night Out, I had the opportunity to see the new regional SWAT vehicle that Reps. Marcell, Tomlinson, and Hogan and I helped secure $350,000 in state funding to purchase. The vehicle has been delivered and housed in Bensalem and is expected to enter service soon. It will serve all three Bucks County regional special units and tactical SWAT teams, helping drastically reduce response times\nUpdates to U.S. 1 Improvement Projects\nPennDOT is encouraging the public to view recent updates to the project website for the upcoming\nU.S. 1 Section RC3 Project\n, to improve safety and congestion along a 2.7-mile section of U.S. 1 in Middletown Township and Langhorne Manor and Langhorne boroughs, Bucks County.\nThe project\nwebsite\nhas been updated with recent project design changes to address comments received during the 45-day comment period on the Environmental Assessment (EA) that concluded on Friday, January 2, 2026. Rep. Hogan and I worked with PennDOT to mak",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Senator Farry's office announced several upcoming constituent events including a home safety event, senior expo, and document shredding event. The release also describes Farry's attendance at National Night Out events, where he presented a state funding check to the Warminster Fire Department for AEDs and highlighted a previously secured $350,000 state-funded regional SWAT vehicle for Bucks County. Additionally, PennDOT updated its project website for a planned U.S. 1 road improvement project in Bucks County.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety",
        "infrastructure",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
