t information for the county director of veterans affairs. They must also notify the county director of veterans affairs of the death of a service person within 96 hours of receiving their remains.\nPennsylvania county veterans affairs offices are available to better assist veterans access benefits for which they might be eligible, and\ntheir contact information can be found here\n.\nHonoring Veterans with Visual Impairments with a Capitol Monument\nA Braille Flag monument honoring veterans with visual impairments could be installed at the Pennsylvania Capitol Building under legislation passed by the Senate and sent to the House of Representatives for consideration.\nThe Braille Flag was created to honor the service of veterans who have been left with life-long visual disabilities. Under\nSenate Bill 1303\n, the monument would be made of bronze and include the Pledge of Allegiance in Braille.\nThe display would be sponsored by the Moose Lodge and the American Braille Flag Project, a non-profit organization dedicated to the placement of these flags throughout the country.\nRead more about Senate Bill 1303 here\n.\nAddressing Veteran Homelessness\nThe U.S. Department of Veterans Affairs’\nHomeless Aging and Disabled Veterans Initiative\naims to help homeless veterans with chronic, complex health needs secure affordable housing within their communities or transition more easily to any higher levels of care they need. The initiative works to better integrate home and community healthcare, social services and permanent supportive housing.\nVeterans experiencing homelessness should visit their local VA Medical Center for assistance. To find a VA Medical Center near you,\nvisit their website here\n. For immediate assistance, contact the National Call Center for Homeless Veterans at (877) 4AID-VET (877-424-3838).\nTo learn more about VA Homeless Programs, click here.\nVA and Steak ‘N Shake Promote Veterans Benefits\nVA has teamed up with Steak ‘n Shake to promote VA benefits and services and make it easier for veterans to become owner-operators of Steak ‘n Shake franchises.\nAs a part of their agreement, Steak ‘n Shake will:\nWaive the $10,000 franchise fee for veterans seeking to run their own franchise under its Franchise Partner Program for Veterans.\nPromote awareness of VA benefits and healthcare on their website and through in-restaurant signage.\nPrioritize hiring veterans and VA beneficiaries, including family, caregivers and survivors for leadership positions across the company.\nFind more information here.\nFree Ray-Ban Meta AI Glasses for Blind Veterans\nMeta is donating\nfree Ray-Ban Meta AI glasses\nto every blind veteran in the U.S.\nVeterans can apply after becoming a\nBlinded Veterans Association (BVA) member\n.\nVeteran organizations can apply to receive glasses for the blinded veterans in their communities here.\nNo-Cost TSA Precheck for Eligible Veterans\nThe VETS (Veterans Expedited TSA Screening) Safe Travel Act was signed into law, allowing certain veterans to\nreceive no-cost TSA PreCheck enrollment\n. TSA ",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Senator Farry's August 2026 newsletter summarizes several recent legislative and government actions related to Pennsylvania veterans, including budget increases for tuition assistance and disability pensions, a new law requiring funeral directors to connect families of deceased service members with county veterans affairs offices, and a Senate-passed bill to install a Braille Flag monument at the state capitol. The newsletter also highlights existing federal programs and corporate initiatives, such as VA homeless services, free Ray-Ban AI glasses for blind veterans, and no-cost TSA PreCheck for eligible veterans.",
